{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W5iEeTqZfNwaJ9wbhT83gJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PFAEv4xEzAdMhVDDnJe4ZT","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an academic paper review. Your focus is **Method and Experiments**. Review the paper described below against top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n\n**Topic:** Compares three execution-free code verification formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = expected output generation / Code World Models, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) under controlled conditions: same model, data, compute. Two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level), multiple seeds.\n\n**Key results:**\n- Function-level: All 3 formulations equivalent (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2%)\n- Repo-level: CLS >> CWM. CLS: 84.8% (Qwen3), 78.3% (DeepSeek). CWM collapses: <30% effective (Qwen3, 93% unparseable), 62.4% (DeepSeek, 52% unparseable)\n- Cross-model: CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% vs 52% unparseable, p<0.001 Fisher)\n- Efficiency: CLS achieves TOST equivalence with TRAJ (p=0.007, ±2pp) at 6-9× speedup\n- Re-repair: CLS diagnostics +1.5pp over control (not significant, p=0.62). Oracle +7.0pp. Negative downstream result.\n\n**Dataset:** SWE-bench PerTest: 34,790 per-test labels from 418/500 SWE-bench Verified instances, 16 candidates each, 72.5% pass rate.\n\n**Method:** All models LoRA rank-16 fine-tuned, 3 epochs, lr=2e-4, cosine schedule, checkpoints selected by val loss at 200-step intervals.\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments from design doc)\n\nThe review context reveals this is the \"median\" tier outcome. Key points:\n- DeepSeek CLS repo-level: 78.3% (seed42), 76.1% (seed123), mean 77.2±1.1%. Best ckpt: step-800, eval_loss 0.38.\n- DeepSeek CWM repo-level: 62.4% overall, 52% unparseable, 71.8% parseable accuracy, 8.3% truncated, mean gen length 2840 tokens.\n- Re-repair: negative result. CLS diag +1.5pp (p=0.62), oracle +7pp, random -6.5pp. n=200, 15% Docker failures.\n- SWE-bench PerTest: 418/500 instances, 16.4% failure rate from Docker issues.\n- Narrative: \"Path A holds but novelty weakened. CLS beats CWM on both model families at repo-level, but DeepSeek CWM unparseable drops from 93% to ~50%, meaning CWM failure is tokenizer-specific not formulation-inherent. Cross-model reversal narrative doesn't hold. Retreat to 'CLS is robust default; CWM failure severity is model-dependent'. Re-repair downstream verification still negative.\"\n\n## Your Review Focus: Method + Experiments\n\nPlease evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design rigor**: Are controls adequate? Is the comparison truly \"controlled\" (same data, compute, model size)? Are there confounds?\n\n2. **Statistical methodology**: TOST equivalence test usage, Fisher's exact test, seed analysis. Are claims properly supported by statistics?\n\n3. **Missing experiments or baselines**: What experiments are missing? (Consider: more seeds, more model families, zero-shot baselines, larger models, constrained decoding for CWM, ablations on LoRA rank, etc.)\n\n4. **Data quality concerns**: SWE-bench PerTest construction, 16.4% failure rate, class imbalance (72.5% pass), Docker reproducibility.\n\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Any over-claims or under-claims?\n\n6. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n7. **Table/figure quality**: Information density, presentation, completeness (note: Table 2 has missing F1_macro values shown as dashes).\n\n8. **Re-repair study**: n=200 with 15% Docker failures. Is this convincing?\n\nOutput your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Method + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\nS3. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. ...\nW2. ...\nW3. ...\nW4. ...\nW5. ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\nQ2. ...\nQ3. ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25187,"cache_read_input_tokens":25239,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25187},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"3c54948a-26a7-4689-bda3-9eeeb4a9d6f4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a00a456173a0bef6d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFAEv4xEzAdMhVDDnJe4ZT","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an academic paper review. Your focus is **Method and Experiments**. Review the paper described below against top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n\n**Topic:** Compares three execution-free code verification formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = expected output generation / Code World Models, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) under controlled conditions: same model, data, compute. Two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level), multiple seeds.\n\n**Key results:**\n- Function-level: All 3 formulations equivalent (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2%)\n- Repo-level: CLS >> CWM. CLS: 84.8% (Qwen3), 78.3% (DeepSeek). CWM collapses: <30% effective (Qwen3, 93% unparseable), 62.4% (DeepSeek, 52% unparseable)\n- Cross-model: CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% vs 52% unparseable, p<0.001 Fisher)\n- Efficiency: CLS achieves TOST equivalence with TRAJ (p=0.007, ±2pp) at 6-9× speedup\n- Re-repair: CLS diagnostics +1.5pp over control (not significant, p=0.62). Oracle +7.0pp. Negative downstream result.\n\n**Dataset:** SWE-bench PerTest: 34,790 per-test labels from 418/500 SWE-bench Verified instances, 16 candidates each, 72.5% pass rate.\n\n**Method:** All models LoRA rank-16 fine-tuned, 3 epochs, lr=2e-4, cosine schedule, checkpoints selected by val loss at 200-step intervals.\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments from design doc)\n\nThe review context reveals this is the \"median\" tier outcome. Key points:\n- DeepSeek CLS repo-level: 78.3% (seed42), 76.1% (seed123), mean 77.2±1.1%. Best ckpt: step-800, eval_loss 0.38.\n- DeepSeek CWM repo-level: 62.4% overall, 52% unparseable, 71.8% parseable accuracy, 8.3% truncated, mean gen length 2840 tokens.\n- Re-repair: negative result. CLS diag +1.5pp (p=0.62), oracle +7pp, random -6.5pp. n=200, 15% Docker failures.\n- SWE-bench PerTest: 418/500 instances, 16.4% failure rate from Docker issues.\n- Narrative: \"Path A holds but novelty weakened. CLS beats CWM on both model families at repo-level, but DeepSeek CWM unparseable drops from 93% to ~50%, meaning CWM failure is tokenizer-specific not formulation-inherent. Cross-model reversal narrative doesn't hold. Retreat to 'CLS is robust default; CWM failure severity is model-dependent'. Re-repair downstream verification still negative.\"\n\n## Your Review Focus: Method + Experiments\n\nPlease evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design rigor**: Are controls adequate? Is the comparison truly \"controlled\" (same data, compute, model size)? Are there confounds?\n\n2. **Statistical methodology**: TOST equivalence test usage, Fisher's exact test, seed analysis. Are claims properly supported by statistics?\n\n3. **Missing experiments or baselines**: What experiments are missing? (Consider: more seeds, more model families, zero-shot baselines, larger models, constrained decoding for CWM, ablations on LoRA rank, etc.)\n\n4. **Data quality concerns**: SWE-bench PerTest construction, 16.4% failure rate, class imbalance (72.5% pass), Docker reproducibility.\n\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Any over-claims or under-claims?\n\n6. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n7. **Table/figure quality**: Information density, presentation, completeness (note: Table 2 has missing F1_macro values shown as dashes).\n\n8. **Re-repair study**: n=200 with 15% Docker failures. Is this convincing?\n\nOutput your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Method + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\nS3. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. ...\nW2. ...\nW3. ...\nW4. ...\nW5. ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\nQ2. ...\nQ3. ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"d19d97e4-5927-4b99-b512-7e346be3d064","session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f"}
