{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MZKo9Zfenc3M26tt7SNedc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tLLM-based automated program repair systems increasingly rely on test-time compute scaling: generating $K$ candidate patches and selecting the best via a verifier~\\cite{snell2024scaling}. Full execution verification is reliable but expensive (\\$1--3 per candidate at repository scale\\footnote{Based on per-instance costs reported by \\citet{yang2024sweagent} and \\citet{xia2024agentless}.}), driving demand for execution-free alternatives. Three distinct formulations have emerged for this task: \\textbf{generation-based simulation}~\\cite{meta2025cwm,maimon2026selfexecution} predicts execution output strings per test; \\textbf{trajectory-level scoring}~\\cite{shum2025swerm} predicts a binary patch verdict without per-test decomposition; and \\textbf{per-test classification}~\\cite{ni2023lever,chen2023codet} predicts a pass/fail token per test. Each makes different tradeoffs between diagnostic granularity, output complexity, and inference cost.\n5\t\n6\tDespite active development along each axis, no prior work provides a controlled comparison holding model capacity, training data, and compute constant. Practitioners choosing a formulation for their verification pipeline must rely on results from different models, different datasets, and different evaluation protocols. Whether one formulation is uniformly preferable, or whether the choice depends on properties of the code being verified, remains an open empirical question. A related question is whether formulation choice matters at all relative to more basic decisions: at what complexity level does fine-tuning become necessary, and does formulation selection add value beyond what fine-tuning alone provides?\n7\t\n8\tWe present the first systematic comparison of these three formulations across two model families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels (function-level and repository-level), and multiple seeds. Our results reveal a three-layer structure in the verification design space. Repository-level verification is inherently harder than function-level (0-shot accuracy drops below majority baseline). Fine-tuning is necessary under standard prompting (0-shot$\\to$fine-tuned gains of 25--35\\pp{} at repo level). Even after fine-tuning, \\emph{formulation choice is governed by a formulation $\\times$ model $\\times$ complexity interaction}: at function-level complexity, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy; at repository-level complexity, formulations diverge sharply, with the divergence being formulation-inherent in direction but model-contingent in magnitude. Figure~\\ref{fig:reformulation} illustrates the three formulations.\n9\t\n10\tWe make the following contributions:\n11\t\n12\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n13\t\\item \\textbf{Formulation equivalence at low complexity.} On function-level benchmarks, \\cls{}, \\cwm{}, and \\traj{} achieve near-identical accuracy across two model families (Qwen3-4B: 88.4/87.2/86.3\\%, 5 seeds; DeepSeek-6.7B: 86.9/86.8\\%, 2 seeds). The \\cls{}--\\cwm{} gap is 0.02--1.24\\pp{}, within seed noise in both cases. This null result establishes that classification is not inherently easier than generation when execution outputs are short and structured.\n14\t\n15\t\\item \\textbf{Complexity-dependent divergence with formulation $\\times$ model interaction.} At repository-level complexity, \\cls{} achieves 19--26\\pp{} higher accuracy than \\cwm{} on Qwen3. Cross-model validation with DeepSeek-Coder confirms the divergence is formulation-inherent: both families show \\cwm{} $\\gg$ \\cls{} degradation from function to repo level (Qwen3: ${\\sim}$5/22\\pp{}; DeepSeek: ${\\sim}$12/25\\pp{} for \\cls{}/\\cwm{}). The magnitude is model-contingent: DeepSeek exhibits larger \\cls{} degradation (${\\sim}$12\\pp{} vs.\\ ${\\sim}$5\\pp{}). %%SCENARIO-A%% Beyond accuracy, \\cwm{} exhibits higher seed sensitivity at repo level (${\\sim}$5\\pp{} across seeds vs.\\ ${\\sim}$2\\pp{} for \\cls{}), while showing no overtraining under our protocol. %%END-SCENARIO-A%%\n16\t\n17\t\\item \\textbf{Error complementarity and ensemble potential.} \\cls{} and \\cwm{} exhibit systematically opposite error patterns at function level: \\cls{} over-predicts passing (75.5\\% of errors are false negatives) while \\cwm{} over-predicts failure (83.1\\% false positives). An oracle ensemble achieves 90.63\\% (+3.12\\pp{} over the best single formulation). The formulations appear to capture complementary aspects of code behavior.\n18\t\n19\t\\item \\textbf{Diagnostic granularity at equivalent accuracy.} Per-test classification achieves statistically equivalent variant-level accuracy to trajectory-level scoring (TOST equivalence $p = 0.007$, $\\pm$2\\pp{} margin) while providing per-test diagnostic information at 217$\\times$ higher throughput than generation (1 output token vs.\\ avg 477). Whether this additional granularity translates to downstream improvements (e.g., targeted re-repair) remains an open question.\n20\t\\end{itemize}\n21\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFAEv4xEzAdMhVDDnJe4ZT","session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"e72f5587-139c-456e-922e-07f778f38de8","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:02:25.945Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013k6N4WsXa4v6ahaZqfW3iR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JKoVjfYQNZUyfadgcCVoF1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/checklist.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9550,"cache_read_input_tokens":31591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9550,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFAEv4xEzAdMhVDDnJe4ZT","session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"7f09cf39-7e74-45ab-8f8b-3cb88177035b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JKoVjfYQNZUyfadgcCVoF1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% ARR Responsible NLP Research Checklist\n2\t% Reference: https://aclrollingreview.org/responsibleNLPresearch/\n3\t\n4\t\\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}\n5\t\n6\tThis paper follows the ARR Responsible NLP Research checklist.\n7\t\n8\t\\subsection*{A. For Every Submission}\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{A1. Did you describe the limitations of your work?}\n11\tYes. Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion} (Limitations paragraph) discusses: single-seed repo-level comparisons, preliminary DeepSeek-Coder \\cwm{} results, synthetic-to-real transfer gap, lack of downstream re-repair validation, and val-test mutation distribution mismatch. Section~\\ref{sec:repo} (Limitations paragraph) discusses val-best checkpoint selection variance and single-seed constraints.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{A2. Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}\n14\tThis work compares formulations for execution-free code verification on public benchmarks. The primary application is improving automated program repair pipelines. We identify no significant risks: the methods predict test outcomes rather than generating executable code, the datasets are derived from open-source repositories, and no personally identifiable information is involved. Misuse potential is limited because the verifiers require task-specific fine-tuning on labeled data and do not generalize to arbitrary code generation.\n15\t\n16\t\\subsection*{B. Did You Use or Create Scientific Artifacts?}\n17\t\n18\t\\paragraph{B1. Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}\n19\tYes. All datasets (HumanEval~\\cite{chen2021humaneval}, MBPP~\\cite{austin2021mbpp}, SWE-bench Verified~\\cite{chowdhury2024swebenchverified}), models (Qwen3~\\cite{qwen2025qwen3}, DeepSeek-Coder~\\cite{guo2024deepseekcoder}), and methods (LoRA~\\cite{hu2022lora}, QLoRA~\\cite{dettmers2024qlora}) are cited in Sections~\\ref{sec:setup} and~\\ref{sec:formulations}.\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{B2. Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}\n22\tHumanEval is released under MIT license; MBPP under CC-BY-4.0; SWE-bench Verified under MIT license. Qwen3 models are released under Apache 2.0; DeepSeek-Coder under the DeepSeek License. All uses are consistent with their respective licenses. We do not release new artifacts in this submission.\n23\t\n24\t\\paragraph{B3. Did you discuss if your use of existing artifact(s) was consistent with their intended use?}\n25\tYes. All benchmarks are used for their intended purpose of evaluating code understanding and generation capabilities. SWE-bench Verified is used for repository-level evaluation as designed by its creators.\n26\t\n27\t\\paragraph{B4. Did you discuss the steps taken to check whether the data contains any information that names or uniquely identifies individual people or offensive content?}\n28\tNot applicable. Our datasets consist of code snippets and test cases from open-source repositories. The mutation-based data construction process operates on source code only and does not involve personal data or natural language content that could contain offensive material.\n29\t\n30\t\\paragraph{B5. Did you provide documentation of the artifacts?}\n31\tYes. Section~\\ref{sec:setup} documents dataset statistics (sizes, splits, label distributions, majority baselines). The mutation-based data construction process is described in Section~\\ref{sec:setup}. Appendix~\\ref{app:hyperparams} provides full hyperparameter details.\n32\t\n33\t\\paragraph{B6. Did you report relevant statistics like the number of examples, details of train/test/dev splits, etc.?}\n34\tYes. Section~\\ref{sec:setup}: function-level 19,076 samples (train 15,290 / val 1,896 / test 1,890; pass:fail $\\approx$ 52:48); repo-level ${\\sim}$70K training, val $n{=}9{,}335$, test $n{=}9{,}784$ (majority baseline 72.96\\%).\n35\t\n36\t\\subsection*{C. Did You Run Computational Experiments?}\n37\t\n38\t\\paragraph{C1. Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure?}\n39\tYes. Models: Qwen3-4B (${\\sim}$4B parameters, LoRA rank 16 $\\approx$ 4M trainable), Qwen3-8B (${\\sim}$8B), DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B (${\\sim}$6.7B). Infrastructure: 4$\\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Total compute: approximately 150 GPU hours across all training runs (${\\sim}$40 configurations including seeds, formulations, model sizes, and ablations). Appendix~\\ref{app:hyperparams} provides per-run details.\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{C2. Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best-found hyperparameter values?}\n42\tYes. Section~\\ref{sec:setup} reports the training configuration (LoRA rank 16, 3 epochs, 5 seeds with mixed precision). Appendix~\\ref{app:hyperparams} provides full hyperparameter values. We use a fixed configuration rather than extensive hyperparameter search; repo-level models use validation accuracy for checkpoint selection.\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{C3. Did you report descriptive statistics about your results with, e.g., error bars or confidence intervals?}\n45\tYes. All multi-seed results report mean $\\pm$ standard deviation. Confidence intervals use clustered bootstrap (114 problem clusters, 10K resamples; Section~\\ref{sec:setup}). Equivalence testing uses TOST with explicit margin sensitivity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}). ICC quantifies within-problem correlation with design effect reported.\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{C4. If you used existing packages, did you report the implementation and version numbers?}\n48\tPartially. We report the use of LoRA~\\cite{hu2022lora} and QLoRA~\\cite{dettmers2024qlora}. Implementation uses HuggingFace Transformers and PEFT libraries. Exact version numbers will be included in the camera-ready and code release.\n49\t\n50\t\\subsection*{D. Did You Use Human Annotators?}\n51\t\n52\tNot applicable. This work uses no human annotators or participants. All labels are derived from automated test execution.\n53\t\n54\t\\subsection*{E. AI Assistants}\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{E1. Did you use AI assistants (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot) in any part of the paper writing or coding process?}\n57\tYes. AI coding assistants were used during development of training and evaluation scripts. AI writing assistants were used for drafting and editing portions of the manuscript. All scientific claims, experimental design, and analysis were conducted and verified by the authors.\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFAEv4xEzAdMhVDDnJe4ZT","session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"5c6f5283-4f8c-42e7-8b4a-ab44cbda844c","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:02:26.535Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W5iEeTqZfNwaJ9wbhT83gJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S9jdEfjtr9TtQWrYKjNWLa","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Review the paper described below against top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n\n**Topic:** Compares three execution-free code verification formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = expected output generation / Code World Models, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) under controlled conditions: same model, data, compute. Two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level), multiple seeds.\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. SWE-bench PerTest dataset (34,790 per-test labels from 418 SWE-bench Verified instances)\n2. Function-level equivalence across formulations (null result)\n3. Complexity-dependent divergence at repo-level (CWM collapses)\n4. Cross-model analysis showing CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% vs 52% unparseable)\n5. Efficiency analysis (6-9× speedup) + diagnostic re-repair study (negative result: +1.5pp, p=0.62)\n\n**Key results:**\n- Function-level: All 3 formulations equivalent (Qwen3: 88.4/87.2/86.3%; DeepSeek: 86.9/86.8/84.2%)\n- Repo-level: CLS >> CWM. CLS: 84.8% (Qwen3), 78.3% (DeepSeek). CWM collapses.\n- Cross-model: CWM unparseable 93% (Qwen3) vs 52% (DeepSeek), p<0.001\n- Re-repair: negative result (CLS +1.5pp, not significant)\n\n**Related Work (3 paragraphs):**\n1. Execution-Free Code Verification: LEVER, CodeT, Code World Models, SWE-RewardModel\n2. Process vs. Outcome Supervision: Lightman et al., Uesato et al. (math reasoning analogy)\n3. Verification in Code Repair: CodeRL, AlphaCode, Reflexion, Predictive mutation testing\n\n**References:** 10 total references cited.\n\n**Method:** LoRA rank-16 fine-tuning on Qwen3-8B and DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B. 3 epochs, lr=2e-4, cosine schedule.\n\n## Review Context\n\nThe narrative guidance from the design doc states: \"Path A holds but novelty weakened. CLS beats CWM on both model families at repo-level, but the cross-model reversal narrative doesn't hold. Retreat to 'CLS is robust default; CWM failure severity is model-dependent'. Re-repair downstream verification still negative. Overall framing publishable but predictable.\"\n\n## Your Review Focus: Novelty + Related Work\n\nPlease evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the core contribution (controlled formulation comparison) genuinely novel? Is the finding that \"CLS > CWM at repo-level because of unparseable outputs\" surprising or predictable? Does the cross-model tokenizer analysis add meaningful insight?\n\n2. **Significance of contributions**: \n   - Is a null result (function-level equivalence) a contribution?\n   - Is demonstrating that CWM fails due to format issues (not semantic capability) a useful insight?\n   - The re-repair study is negative. Does this weaken or strengthen the paper?\n\n3. **Related work completeness**: With only 10 references, is the coverage adequate? Missing areas might include:\n   - Constrained decoding / structured generation literature\n   - More recent code verification papers (2024-2025)\n   - Execution prediction literature beyond CWM\n   - Test generation / mutation testing\n   - LLM-as-judge approaches for code\n\n4. **Positioning**: Does the paper clearly position itself relative to Code World Models (the most direct comparison)? Does it address the scale difference (their 32B mid-trained vs. this paper's 8B LoRA)?\n\n5. **Framing quality**: Is the \"design space\" framing appropriate or overstated? (Only 3 formulations, 2 models, no architectural exploration, no training strategy variations.)\n\n6. **Writing quality**: Assess abstract, introduction narrative, overall paper structure. Any AI-writing markers (excessive dashes, -ing phrases, etc.)?\n\n7. **Incremental vs. substantial**: For a top ML venue, does this clear the bar? Or is it \"well-executed but predictable\"?\n\nOutput your review in this exact format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\nS3. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. ...\nW2. ...\nW3. ...\nW4. ...\nW5. ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\nQ2. ...\nQ3. ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25187,"cache_read_input_tokens":25239,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25187},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"45a370c9-5617-4f5b-b04a-999e3b7fef4d"}
