{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016BxqhfJXmM7r7KERT9kRTk","type":"tool_result","content":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/related_work.tex:22:Lightman et al.~\\cite{lightman2023letsverify} demonstrate that process-based reward models (verifying each reasoning step) outperform outcome-based models (verifying only the final answer) for mathematical reasoning.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/related_work.tex:29:AlphaCode~\\cite{li2022alphacode} and AlphaCode 2~\\cite{alphacode2023team} use massive sampling with test-based filtering for competition programming.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/related_work.tex:35:\\paragraph{Predictive Mutation Testing.}\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/related_work.tex:40:Both have become standard for code-specific fine-tuning. DeepSeek-Coder~\\cite{guo2024deepseekcoder} shows that adapter-based training preserves pretrained code understanding and specializes for downstream tasks.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:13:\\paragraph{Models and training.}\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:60:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:159:{\\footnotesize \\textsuperscript{$\\dagger$}Two seeds (42 / 123). \\textsuperscript{$\\star$}\\cwm{} running accuracy; final values pending. \\cwm{} parseable-only accuracy: 42.88\\% (4B), 38.52\\% (8B).}\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:187:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:190:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:200:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:206:Relaxed matching (tolerating format deviations, L0--L4) \\emph{decreases} \\cwm{} accuracy: 58.04\\%$\\to$56.98\\% (seed 42) and 59.09\\%$\\to$55.91\\% (seed 123). Unparseable predictions default to the majority class (pass, 72.96\\%), which outperforms the model's own parseable predictions (42--53\\%). The divergence widens from 26\\pp{} to 28\\pp{} under relaxed matching. This rules out the hypothesis that parsing strictness inflates the observed gap.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:217:\\paragraph{(g) LoRA rank and majority voting.}\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/experiments.tex:221:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/appendix.tex:66:\\paragraph{Majority voting.}\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/appendix.tex:125:Table~\\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists all hyperparameters used for fine-tuning. Both \\cls{} and \\cwm{} share the same LoRA configuration, optimizer, and training schedule; the only differences are per-device batch size, gradient accumulation steps (adjusted to maintain identical effective batch size of 32 across model sizes), and learning rate (DeepSeek-Coder uses $2{\\times}10^{-4}$ vs.\\ $5{\\times}10^{-5}$ for Qwen3, tuned on a held-out validation split).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/introduction.tex:8:[Omitted long matching line]\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/abstract.tex:2:Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated in multiple ways, yet no controlled comparison exists across formulations at matched model capacity. We systematically evaluate three formulations: per-test binary classification (\\cls{}), execution output generation (\\cwm{}), and trajectory-level patch scoring, training on identical data with controlled model sizes (4B, 8B).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/checklist.tex:34:Yes. Section~\\ref{sec:setup}: function-level 19,076 samples (train 15,290 / val 1,896 / test 1,890; pass:fail $\\approx$ 52:48); repo-level ${\\sim}$70K training, val $n{=}9{,}335$, test $n{=}9{,}784$ (majority baseline 72.96\\%).\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/conclusion.tex:4:We presented the first controlled comparison of three formulations for execution-free code repair verification: per-test binary classification (\\cls{}), execution output generation (\\cwm{}), and trajectory-level scoring. Our central finding is that no single formulation dominates across all conditions; the design space is governed by a formulation $\\times$ model $\\times$ complexity interaction.\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/conclusion.tex:16:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01S9jdEfjtr9TtQWrYKjNWLa","session_id":"2ec43eb0-267d-4891-8215-c57a95dc449f","uuid":"419c1b90-2d1b-4c71-8442-4fdf2c83ba64","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:03:00.307Z"}
